    ] },
    { "as": "Ann", "type": "examine pillars", "expect": [
      { "reader": "Ann", "kind": "described", "words": "Mighty stone pillars rise from the floor of the gorge to hold the arches up, each one the work of a year and a hundred men; some stand as they stood, and some are starting to crumble from age, and the arches they hold have begun to lean. One of them has gone: a heap of smoke and rubble at the foot of the gorge, and above it, where an arch was, a gap with the far side of the channel standing in the air." }
    ] },
    { "as": "Ann", "type": "examine aqueduct", "expect": [
      { "reader": "Ann", "kind": "described", "words": "The aqueduct is large and impressive. It was probably the major method of water transport in the Empire. It strides from north to south across the gorge on arches that would hold up a city, and did; and here and there a pillar has begun to go, and the arch above it with it, and the channel on top carries nothing now but moss and a little rain. It is the saddest thing you have seen underground, and you have seen some sad things." }
    ] }
  ]
